			<div class="content" id="post-content-4849729">
									

<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Genre (Optional):</strong> Science Fiction</p>
<p><strong>Page Layout (Optional):</strong> Standard SCP article.</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> There's an alien ecosystem in Kraken Mare, the largest of Titan's liquid methane seas. The anomaly is that the life there isn't really alien at all - all the organisms there are copies of marine life from Earth's Cretaceous seas, modified on the chemical level to survive Titan's conditions but unchanged on the macro scale. So rather than aliens swimming in methane, there are mosasaurs and ammonites instead.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> The central narrative is the mystery of how prehistoric animals ended up living on Titan. The Foundation first found the anomaly when a NASA probe to Kraken Mare got eaten - containment focuses on keeping information from getting out rather than doing anything on Titan itself. Eventually the Foundation sends another probe to explore Kraken Mare. The exploration log ends with a plinth on the seafloor that lists the surface liquids and basic biochemical reactions found on Earth, Titan, and an unknown third world with sulfuric acid seas. The implication is that aliens copy-pasted an ecosystem from one liquid medium to another, and did the same elsewhere, for reasons unknown.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> I think it's a fun mix between aliens and dinosaurs, combining real-life sea monsters with exotic hypothetical biochemistry. The story also ends with the revelation that powerful anomalous beings once came to Earth and, for once, did something pretty benign.</p>
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong> I'm thinking about having the plinth be written in Ortothan, just to tie the SCP into something bigger and make it feel more connected to some other space SCPs.</p>

							</div>

								
					
